---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { people, dead } from "../lib/people.js";
import stats from "../data/stats.json";
import Plates from "../components/Plates.astro";
const here = people.filter((p) => [p.birthPlace, p.deathPlace, p.burialPlace]
  .some((x) => (x || "").startsWith("Piedimonte")));
/* The Mazza of this town, and how many of them the 10 September join brought in.
   THIS USED TO READ `p.comp === 2`, AND THAT WAS A BUG WITH A PUBLISHED FACE.
   Component numbers are assigned by iteration order and they move: on 10
   September the documented join of Rosario Mazza merged the hundred and ten
   into the main component, and `comp === 2` silently came to mean SEVEN
   MAZZITELLI OF NEW YORK. This page went on calling them «Mazza of Piedimonte
   Etneo — Mariano, Leonardo, Orazio…» and tabling the Mazzitelli underneath,
   for thirteen days. Select people by who they are, never by a number the
   builder is free to reshuffle — the same lesson the slug ledger learned. */
const mazza = people.filter((p) => p.name.split(" ").pop() === "Mazza");
const joined = mazza.filter((p) => p.comp === 1);
const stillApart = mazza.filter((p) => p.comp !== 1);
const earliestMazza = Math.min(...mazza.filter((p) => p.born).map((p) => p.born));
---
<Base title="Piedimonte Etneo" eyebrow="Catania, Sicily"
      dek="The town on the north-east flank of Etna that gave this family its surname — and, of the four quarters, the one the archive knows least about.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{here.length}</div><div class="l">people placed at Piedimonte Etneo</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{earliestMazza}</div><div class="l">the earliest Mazza birth with a date</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{stillApart.length}</div><div class="l">Mazza still joined to nothing</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The town</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><em>Piedimonte Etneo</em> means the foot of the mountain, and it is exactly that: a comune of
      the province of Catania standing on the north-eastern slope of <strong>Etna</strong>, above
      Fiumefreddo and the Alcantara valley, looking out at the Ionian Sea. The neighbouring towns in
      this archive — <strong>Mascali</strong>, <strong>Giarre</strong>, <strong>Linguaglossa</strong>'s
      side of the mountain — are all within a few kilometres.</p>
      <p>Living on Etna is a recurring administrative fact, not a metaphor. In
      <strong>1928</strong> the eruption that destroyed the town of Mascali — the only town Etna has
      swallowed whole in modern times — happened eight years after <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> was
      born here, and the archive records Mascali as a birthplace for people in this family.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The Sicilian quarter is four towns, not one</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Piedimonte Etneo gave the family its surname. It did
    not give it everybody.</p>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>For most of this archive's life the Sicilian side was a single town, and the family tree
      still says so: it puts <P n="Maria Nicotra" /> at Piedimonte Etneo, where her husband was born.
      <strong>Four consecutive documents, read in September 2026, take her and her father somewhere
      else</strong> — and the towns are all within eight kilometres of each other, down the same
      coast road.</p>
      <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink-2);padding-left:20px">
        <li><strong>Piedimonte Etneo</strong> — the <strong>Mazza</strong>, back to Mariano Mazza and
        Maria Catalano, both alive and working there in April 1871. Also where
        <strong>Venera Cassaniti</strong> was born in 1848, and where she and Giovanni Nicotra
        married on 7 October 1872.</li>
        <li><strong>Mascali</strong> — where Giovanni Nicotra was living in 1872, and where
        <strong>Maria Nicotra was born on 8 December 1875</strong>, at half past nine in the evening,
        in <em>contrada Sopra Pietrafitta</em>. Her birth act was found here after fourteen
        consecutive Piedimonte birth indexes had been read without her, because a wife went to her
        husband's comune and the children were registered there.</li>
        <li><strong>Giarre</strong> — where the 1872 publication certificate says Giovanni Nicotra
        himself was <em>nato</em>. <strong>Eleven Giarre birth years, 1838 to 1848, have been read end
        to end, main runs and appendices, and they do not hold him.</strong> For a day this archive
        let that stand for the town and wrote that Giarre's only Nicotra was a <em>Dottor Don</em>.
        Then its <strong>first</strong> year was opened: <strong>1820 holds two untitled Nicotra
        households</strong>, and one of the fathers is a Rosario. The decade was wrong, not the town.</li>
        <li><strong>Riposto</strong> — the lower town, which has registered separately from Giarre
        since <strong>1821</strong>. Twenty-three Riposto years are now read, births and marriages,
        and Giovanni is in none of them. What Riposto gave instead is the shape of the answer:
        <strong>its 1844 marriage Tavola holds a Nicotra born at Giarre who married here and
        registered his children here</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">None of that was in the family's export, and none of it changes who
      Michael Rocco Mazza's grandparents were. It changes where his great-grandmother was born, and
      it adds three comuni and about two thousand volumes to what this archive has to read. Her own
      birth act is <a href={u("/mascali/")}>reproduced on the Mascali page</a>.
      <a href={u("/nicotra/")}>The Nicotra line →</a> · <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been read →</a></p>
    </div>

    {/* Drawn to scale from the coordinates in data/gazetteer-local.json, which
        were looked up against OpenStreetMap rather than estimated. The strip is
        tall and narrow because that is the shape of the ground: these towns are
        strung along ten kilometres of coast between Etna and the Ionian Sea,
        and a square diagram would be a lie about how close they are. */}
    <figure style="margin:2rem 0 0">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 400 585" width="100%" style="max-width:420px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto"
           role="img" aria-label="The four Sicilian comuni of this archive, drawn to scale: Piedimonte Etneo inland to the north, then Mascali, then Riposto on the coast and Giarre just inland of it — all within about ten kilometres.">
        <defs>
          <linearGradient id="sea" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="0">
            <stop offset="0" stop-color="currentColor" stop-opacity="0.05"/>
            <stop offset="1" stop-color="currentColor" stop-opacity="0.16"/>
          </linearGradient>
        </defs>
        <g style="color:var(--ink-3)">
          <rect x="262" y="0" width="138" height="585" fill="url(#sea)"/>
          <text x="330" y="300" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor"
                font-style="italic" opacity="0.75" style="font-family:var(--serif)">Ionian Sea</text>
          {/* Etna, to the west — the reason the towns sit where they do */}
          <path d="M6 96 L44 30 L82 96 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.35"/>
          <text x="44" y="112" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" opacity="0.6"
                style="font-family:var(--sans)">Etna</text>
          {/* the road down the coast */}
          <path d="M96 76 L181 334 L211 491 L138 519" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 4" opacity="0.4"/>
          {/* scale bar: 50px = 1 km */}
          <g opacity="0.55">
            <line x1="20" y1="556" x2="120" y2="556" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="20" y1="551" x2="20" y2="561" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="120" y1="551" x2="120" y2="561" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="70" y="574" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor"
                  style="font-family:var(--sans)">2 km</text>
          </g>
        </g>
        {[
          { x: 96,  y: 76,  n: "Piedimonte Etneo", s: "the Mazza", c: "var(--ink)" },
          { x: 181, y: 334, n: "Mascali",          s: "Maria Nicotra born 1875", c: "var(--ink)" },
          { x: 211, y: 491, n: "Riposto",          s: "the Nicotra households", c: "var(--ink)" },
          { x: 138, y: 519, n: "Giarre",           s: "«nato a Giarre» — and not there", c: "var(--ink)" },
        ].map((p) => (
          <g>
            <circle cx={p.x} cy={p.y} r="5.5" fill={p.c}/>
            <text x={p.x + 12} y={p.y - 1} font-size="13" fill="var(--ink)"
                  style="font-family:var(--serif)">{p.n}</text>
            <text x={p.x + 12} y={p.y + 14} font-size="11" fill="var(--ink-3)"
                  style="font-family:var(--sans)">{p.s}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:10px;text-align:center">Ten kilometres, four comuni, four
      separate sets of registers. <strong>Giarre and Riposto are fifteen hundred metres apart</strong> and
      have registered separately since 1821 — which is the whole difficulty.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The line that carries the name, and stops</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Three men, and then nothing.</p>
    <div class="rec">
      <div class="k">Generation 3</div>
      <div class="v"><P n="Rosario Mazza" /> — no dates, no places, no record. He exists only because
      his son's record names him.</div><div class="w">—</div>
      <div class="k">Generation 2</div>
      <div class="v"><P n="Salvatore Mazza" /> — born at Piedimonte Etneo, died at Catania.
      Married <P n="Maria Nicotra" />.</div><div class="w">1879–1929</div>
      <div class="k">Generation 1</div>
      <div class="v"><P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> — born here, arrived Fremantle 4 October 1949,
      died at Brisbane and buried at <a href={u("/nudgee/")}>Nudgee</a>.</div><div class="w">1920–2002</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">Four generations. It is the shortest of the
    <a href={u("/lines/")}>four quarters</a> by five generations, and the surname the whole archive is
    named after runs out at a man about whom precisely one fact is known: that he was Salvatore's
    father.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <div class="lab">10 September 2026 · the archive's largest open question, closed</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The hundred and ten, joined</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">For a while this page said the opposite, and went on
    saying it for thirteen days after it stopped being true.</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The family tree used to hold <strong>two Mazza kindreds that touched nowhere</strong>: the
      line down to Sebastiano, and a separate island of a hundred and ten Piedimonte Mazza —
      Mariano, Leonardo, Orazio, Umberto, Giuseppa, Serena, Graziella and the rest — with parents,
      children, marriages and cousins of their own, and <em>not one record joining them</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>Five records closed it.</strong> The export carried Salvatore Mazza's undated father
      Rosario twice: once as a stub, once as «Rosario Mazza b. 1850 of Piedimonte Etneo» at the head
      of the island. <em>Nati</em> 1873 atto 3 names Salvatore's father <strong>«Rosario Mazza di
      Mariano», 28</strong>, and his mother Leonarda Bonanno; marriage proclamations of 1898, 1902
      and 1910 give Rosario's age again — 53, 55, 65 — and name three more sons. The island's Rosario
      has exactly those sons by a wife called Leonarda. <strong>One man.</strong></p>
      <p>So <strong>{joined.length} of the {mazza.length} Mazza in this archive now sit in one
      connected tree</strong>, and {stillApart.length} do not. The join is not an automatic merge —
      the automatic rules refused it, correctly, after an earlier build fused four undated Rosario
      Mazza on the name alone. It is <strong>declared</strong>, in <code>documented-joins.tsv</code>,
      with its citation and with what would overturn it. <strong>An automatic merge is a rule; a
      documented join is an argument, and an argument has to be published so it can be
      attacked.</strong></p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Why this section was wrong until 23 September.</strong>
      It selected its people by <em>component number</em> — <code>comp === 2</code> — and component
      numbers move. When the join merged the island into the main tree, <code>comp === 2</code> came
      to mean <strong>seven Mazzitelli of New York</strong>, and this page tabled them under a
      heading about the Mazza of Piedimonte Etneo while its prose named Mariano, Leonardo and Orazio.
      Nothing was lost and no record was wrong; a page simply went on describing a question after the
      archive had answered it. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The open questions →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Plates see="/piedimonte-etneo" title="The act itself" />
</Base>
